{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>איש (a man).</b> The sense of <b>ואשר יתן ממנו על זר (and whoever puts of it on a stranger).</b> One who is not of the family of Aaron. The Gaon [Saadia] said: there was a tradition in Israel's hands that David and his seed would be anointed with the anointing oil, and no other kings. And he needed to explain thus because Zadok took of the anointing oil that was in the Tent of Meeting and anointed Solomon with it. Yefet said that על זר means one who is not of the children of Israel, but he did not speak correctly, for behold: והזר הקרב יומת (Numbers 3:10), and its interpretation is למען אשר לא יקרב איש זר אשר לא מזרע אהרן הוא (ibid. 17:5). And others said: concerning the anointing oil that Moses made. This too does not seem correct in my eyes, for at the beginning it is written: שמן משחת קדש יהיה זה לי לדרתיכם (Exodus 30:31), and furthermore: איש אשר ירקח כמוהו — and whoever gives of that which is כמוהו upon a זר. Now there is no difference between the anointing oil that Moses made and that made afterward, which is כמוהו. And exclusion of the family of David is not written. And in my opinion, all the commandments are eternal truths for the generations of Israel, just as burnt offerings may not be offered except in the chosen place — yet Gideon and Samuel, and also Elijah on Mount Carmel, offered burnt offerings. And I mentioned Gideon because he offered burnt offerings and is not a priest, and likewise Samuel, for it would not avail even if he were of the sons of Korah. Now all of these were instances of temporary dispensation; likewise we may say regarding Solomon's affairs, that by temporary dispensation through the hand of Nathan the prophet or Gad he was anointed with the anointing oil."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:39:33.009323691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}